cks the King but forces **K x f7**. White sacrifices a powerful Knight for a check that drives the King to a safer square, diminishing the attack.\n3. **Q: f6-g7**: This move is a quiet "waiting" move that threatens inevitable checkmate on the next turn via **Q: g7-f8#**.\n   - The square f8 is attacked by the Queen (from g7) and supported by the Bishop at h6.\n   - If White plays Q-f8+, the King cannot capture the Queen (protected by Bishop), cannot move to d8 (protected by Knight f7), cannot move to d7 (protected by Bishop b5), and cannot move to e7 or capture the Knight at f7 (both covered by the Queen at f8).\n   - Black has no piece that can defend f8 or attack the Queen at g7. Even if Black moves the Knight at e7 to try and clear a square, the mate or decisive advantage remains.\n\nReason: The move **Q: f6-g7** sets up an unstoppable mate threat at f8. Unlike the immediate checks which allow the King to flee, this move seals the King\'s fate by taking away any future escape routes and utilizing the coordination of White\'s Queen, Knight, and Bishop perfectly.\n\nAnswer: Q: f6-g7'}
